AB  - Coupled oscillator networks are drawing attention as machine-learning substrates while physics, neuroscience and neuromorphic computing converge on the same oscillatory dynamics from different directions. Physics describes synchronization regimes with computational properties rather than mere order; neuroscience finds functional oscillatory properties in the neuron; and machine learning has produced oscillator architectures that classify, generate images and navigate, with evidence that oscillatory neural networks (ONNs) can be trained, learn, remember and reason. We hypothesize that a substrate whose native operations are resonance and entrainment resembles the neurons evolution refined to sense physical signals, and that a trainable model built from those dynamics would make learning, forgetting and rhythm disruption addressable in simulation. This survey does not test that hypothesis, but uses it for motivation to audit the ONN landscape. We organize eighteen published systems, on two primary questions: whether gradients reach the oscillator dynamics, and whether a conventional encoder or decoder is trained around it. We record the task, data, controls and comparison with conventional networks at comparable budgets; identifying notable findings and methodology gaps. We found the dynamics were rarely isolated; the coupling term has only been removed as a single variable three times, once without a reported number, once on a single dataset, and once only as a retrained model; and no report varies coupling range or boundary conditions as a single variable. We close with eight directions toward shared data, model evaluations and improved controls.
